member.remarks:
The rounds loaded are a large figure that rolls to each new count as an odometer does, the rounds carried beside it smaller;
under them the magazine is a row of pips, lit for each round still in it. A change of weapon slides the block in from the
right and sweeps its rule across; a reload runs an amber bar along the rule.

Not retail. The glass tint, bloom and bezel vignette are a resolve-time display transform (see
) and the marked bodies are silhouetted over the picture (see
); this draws the crisp vector interface on top: thin rails and corner ticks, the compass
strip, the rangefinder reticle, a chevron and range over each contact, and the magnification and range. The faithful
retail overlays stay each title's own.

member.param:
Whether this is one of the surface cache's coarse maps (see
) rather than a lamp shadow map. Those are drawn once and
kept, so they hold the static world alone: a soldier drawn into one would stand there for good.

member.remarks:
The tactical computer and the binocular optics draw the same family of marks, so the geometry lives
here once rather than being copied into each renderer. Everything takes already-scaled pixel values;
callers apply  before handing sizes in.
